\f1\fs22 Sarah got up from the sofa like a seal discovering it had legs, went to the door and peeled the blinds aside. Hayley and Kathy stood outside, both making faces of distress, disbelief and probably a few more disses besides.\
Sarah flicked the lock and Hayley slid the door back.\
\'93Can you believe it?\'94 Hayley said as she passed through.\
\'93Yes,\'94 Sarah said then lowered herself back onto the sofa.\
Kathy sat too. Hayley couldn\'92t; she paced around the room, absently touching things as Alexander\'92s show played on the wall-mounted TV.\
\'93How did you get out?\'94 Sarah asked.\
\'93Guards are too busy watching the show on their devices.\'94\
\'93What are you doing?\'94 Sarah demanded of the Alexander on the screen. \'93I sent you to get Chip, not stage a coup! I thought you could at least manage that.\'94\
\'93Apparently he has,\'94 Kathy said.\
\'93And put Eloise in his place.\'94 Sarah twisted, trying to find the internal organ she\'92d prefer Little Clinton to lean on. \'93I should have been more specific.\'94\
\'93He won\'92t hurt her,\'94 Sarah said. \'93Everything about Alexander is story. None of it is real.\'94\
Hayley and Kathy exchanged awkward glances.\
\'93People can\'92t just go missing,\'94 Kathy reasoned. \'93There\'92d be investigations.\'94\
Hayley looked grimmer. \'93Truth is, as long as Cloudborne stay below the Layer, they\'92re mostly left to police themselves.\'94\
Sarah nodded. She\'92d rarely seen police presence in her nights of Elvi bars. Or for long before that.\
\'93Alexander\'92s a dick, but he\'92s not a monster,\'94 Kathy reasoned.\
\'93A person can be two things at once,\'94 Hayley said, with the gravity of professional experience.\
Kathy picked at a patch. \'93I just assumed they\'92d send them home with a months\'92 worth of breakfast cereal from a sponsor. Perhaps a tracksuit.\'94\
\'93Sure,\'94 Hayley said. \'93With a t-shirt that says, 
\f2\i I Got the Trebuchet Slap
\f1\i0 .\'94\
\'93They\'92ve actually got those on their merch store,\'94 Kathy answered.\
They watched the TV for a few seconds.\
\'93He\'92s really doing a number on the Sages,\'94 Sarah observed. \'93I should never have allowed them in.\'94\
After a moment, Kathy said,  \'93Eloise must still have forces loyal to her.\'94\
Hayley looked grim. \'93Did you see that audience? That\'92s a significant chunk of the security personnel right there. You\'92d be surprised how automated Sublime Point defences are.\'94\
\'93Alexander can\'92t have turned all of them,\'94 Kathy said.\
\'93No, but I wouldn\'92t be planning on trusting any of them,\'94 Hayley answered.\
Kathy nodded. \'93The UDF at the gates probably won\'92t treat Eloise any better.\'94\
\'93And the Elvi?\'94 Hayley started. \'93They\'92ll protect you,\'94 she said to Sarah, and left the rest hanging.\
Sarah\'92s lips thinned. \'93We have to go get her.\'94\
Hayley ran her fingers through her hair. \
Kathy checked her pockets, perhaps searching for her best doctor\'92s advice tone. \'93You\'92re not going anywhere,\'94 she told Sarah. \'93You\'92re due in, like, any nanosecond.\'94\
\'93I\'92m going.\'94\
\'93Both of you stay here,\'94 Hayley said. \'93You\'92ll both get in the way.\'94\
\'93Really?\'94 Sarah challenged. \'93I think I have the best chance of influencing him.\'94\
Hayley fumed. She wanted to influence Alexander with a bullet in his head, but she couldn\'92t deliver it in a hall full of gun-toting bromancers out for blood.\
Sarah put both hands on the sofa, ready to lever herself up. \'93I\'92m going.\'94\
Hayley\'92s shoulders fell. \'93Okay. But I\'92ll go ahead. Kathy\'92ll come with you.\'94 She looked at Kathy with a strange mix of command and affection. \'93Make sure she doesn\'92t push herself.\'94\
\'93\'91Push\'92 is an unfortunate word here,\'94 Kathy said. \'93Let\'92s just hope I won\'92t be delivering Little Clinton in the middle of the road.\'94
